\paragraph{Further baselines.}
S3 (a greedy wrapper that scores each candidate benchmark by the cross-validated error of the factor model on training rows, with a closed-form evaluation for cost), S5 ($k$-medoids on $1-|\text{correlation}|$ of the benchmarks), S6s and S7 (a regression tree and nearest-neighbour interview, run on continuous scores rather than ratings as in \citealt{golbandi2011adaptive}) were run on 20 of the 50 splits with the factor model for inference, on the same 154 models; S3 and S7 only for $k\le32$ because of their cost. The paired gain over random selection (points; the same quantity as in Table~\ref{tab:primary_contrast}) is, at $k=1$, 2, 8 and 20: S3 1.90, 1.35, 0.32, $-0.30$; S5 1.49, 1.68, 0.46, 0.25; S7 1.08, 1.11, $-0.13$, $-0.54$; S6 1.68, 1.98, 0.77, 0.49 on these splits (1.58, 1.88, 0.81, 0.49 on all 50); and S6s 1.68, 2.20, 0.79, 0.50.

\paragraph{Greedy against exhaustive.} On the 24 held-out models of the first group fold that have more than 20 observed cells, we compared the greedy set with the best set of the same criterion $F(S)$, over all of a model's observed cells as candidates and all benchmarks as target, with the factor model at the rank chosen for that $k$ (exhaustive for $k=2,3$, a single-swap search from the greedy set for $k=8,20$). Here the error is on the cells left unrun, not on the common target set, and there is no interval. The criterion of the greedy set is worse than the best found by 17.5\% on average at $k=2$ (at most 39\%) and 35\% at $k=3$ (at most 52\%); the two sets coincide for 17\% of models at $k=2$ and for none at $k=3$. The RMSE of the factor model is 10.35 against 9.24 points at $k=2$ and 9.26 against 8.75 at $k=3$ for the greedy and the best set, the better set winning for 46\% and 63\% of the models. With the swap search the gap is 5.2\% at $k=8$ (RMSE 8.52 against 8.44) and 1.7\% at $k=20$ (7.73 against 7.80, the swapped set worse).

