\section{Checks of the method}
\label{app:checks}

\paragraph{Adaptive and batch selection.}
We check Proposition~\ref{prop:t3} numerically on a simulated model with known parameters ($N=60$ benchmarks, $r=4$ factors, $k=6$, 4000 draws of a new model). The greedy sequence is the same for every draw of the scores, and the Monte-Carlo mean squared error of the batch design on the unrun benchmarks, 0.682 (standard error 0.0026), matches the closed form of Proposition~\ref{prop:t1}, 0.683. The proposition needs known parameters, and our adaptive baseline S7 does not use them: on the synthetic ladder (8 seeds, 80 test models per seed, every rule scored on the benchmarks outside the union of the S6 and S7 sets) the designed batch set S6 has the lower squared error on every rung at every $k\ge2$, by 0.04--0.07 at $k=2$, 0.14--0.17 at $k=5$ and 0.03--0.06 at $k=20$ in standardised units (at least 3.1 standard errors). S7 recovers 53\%, 22\% and 14\% of S6's gain over random selection at $k=1$, 2 and 5 (mean over rungs). This is a statement about this untuned neighbour-tree rule and not about adaptive design in general.

\paragraph{Synthetic data.}
\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.62\linewidth]{../figtab/fig3c_estimation_vs_M.pdf}
\caption{Estimation term of the risk on synthetic data (separated factors, correct model): the measured error minus the error with the true parameters against the number of training models $M$ at $k=1$, 5 and 20, with least-squares fits $c/M$; the log-log slope is $-1.26$ to $-1.15$ over $k=1$ to 32.}
\label{fig:estimation}
\end{figure}
The synthetic studies of Section~\ref{sec:experiments} use $N=120$ benchmarks and a factor model with intercepts $\mathcal N(0,1)$ and noise variances $\psi_j=\bigl(U(0.3,1)\bigr)^2\times0.35\times$ the mean signal variance. In the separated regime the true rank is 6, with loadings $\mathcal N(0,1/N)\times8$ (equal strengths) and the model fitted at rank 6; in the decaying regime the true rank is 40, with strengths $16\,i^{-0.8}$, and the model fitted at rank 8. Each model observes a share of the benchmarks drawn from $\mathrm{Beta}(2,1.33)$ (mean 0.6). Training sizes are $M\in\{40,80,160,320,640\}$ for the correct separated model and $M=240$ for the decaying regime and the four misspecified rungs (a $\tanh$ link; Student-$t_3$ noise of equal variance; noise scaled by $1+1.5\times$ the clipped upper-tail standardised latent; noise shared within clusters of benchmarks). The test set is 150 fully observed models, the target is the $N-k$ benchmarks not run, random selection averages 20 draws per model, and the figures average 8 seeds; the diagnostics of the ladder use 20 seeds. Columns are standardised by the training mean and standard deviation.

\paragraph{Diagnostics on the other held-out settings.} The six diagnostics of the ladder (Section~\ref{sec:experiments}) and its placement rule, unchanged, applied to the leaderboard (families, 10 folds; strongest quarter) and to the MTEB temporal and strength splits (one outer fold each), place none of them on a rung. Calibration of the predictive variance is 2.8 (MTEB, organisations), 24.8 (temporal), 75.5 (strength), 1.9 (leaderboard, families) and 5.5 (strongest quarter); excess kurtosis of the residuals is 29, 81, 234, 29 and 107. The ratio of the measured floor to the predicted noise is 2.5, 9.6, 7.9, 1.1 and 2.5 in the same order. The MTEB temporal ratio has wide bounds [3.75, 10.7] (4 organisations). Across these five settings the rank correlation of the ratio with calibration, residual correlation and heteroscedasticity is 0.8, 0.8 and 0.9, with kurtosis 0.5 and with the link diagnostic $-1.0$; with five points this is descriptive and not a test. The ladder has 120 benchmarks and 238 training models, so for the leaderboard (39 benchmarks) it is a reference scale and not a matched design.

\paragraph{Fixed factor rank (post hoc).} With the rank fixed at 4, 8 or 12 for every fold and $k$ (headline subset, 20 of the 50 splits, floor at the same rank), the floor is 6.24, 5.64 and 5.22 points, against 4.99 with the rank chosen by inner cross-validation on the same splits (5.06 on all 50). The excess of S6 over the floor at $k=20$ is 0.25, 0.38 and 0.59 points (1.33 with the cross-validated rank), and $k^*$ at the secondary margin is 3, 8 and 20 (32 [20, 50], the same on all 50 splits). The RMSE of S6 at $k=20$ is 6.49, 6.03 and 5.81 points against 6.31. Paired by model, the RMSE of the cross-validated rank minus that of the fixed rank at $k=20$ is $-0.17$ [$-0.28$, $-0.07$] for rank 4, 0.29 [0.18, 0.40] for rank 8 and 0.50 [0.32, 0.69] for rank 12, and at $k=2$ it is $-2.5$ for rank 12; at $k=80$ the cross-validated rank is lowest. So $k^*$ measures the distance to the floor of a model of the same capacity, and a rank chosen with hindsight on the test models is better than the cross-validated one at $k=20$ and 32 by up to 0.6 points (rank 12 at $k=32$: 0.57 [0.43, 0.71]); no rule that avoids the hindsight is claimed.

\paragraph{Leaderboard groups by uploader.} Holding out the 717 uploaders instead of the 475 family stems (G split, same 50 splits, k grid and rank grid; the bootstrap resamples uploaders) gives a floor of 4.79 points (4.82 with families) and a gain of S6 over S0 (both with the factor-model posterior mean) of 2.11, 1.88, 1.35, 0.79, 0.44 and 0.25 points at $k=1$, 2, 3, 5, 8 and 13 (2.10, 1.84, 1.40, 0.77, 0.44, 0.28 with families), within 0.05 point of the family run at every $k$. $k^*$ at $\delta_{\text{floor}}$ is 5 [3, 5] against 3 [3, 5], one grid step, and the excess at $k=3$ (1.12) sits at the margin (1.12). The training-side rule was not re-run on this grouping, and the strongest-quarter split is unchanged.

\section{Compute, licences and use of language models}
\label{app:compute}

All experiments ran on CPU only, on shared containers of four cores, one process per fold with four at a time and single-thread linear algebra. Per outer fold, fitting the factor models and the inner cross-validation took 23 to 27 s on the group split and 54 s on the temporal and strength splits; evaluating S0, S1, S2, S4, S6 and S6T over 50 splits took 54 to 80 s on the group split, 62 s on the temporal and 80 s on the strength split; the additional baselines and inference rules took 51 to 58 s per group fold and 107 to 123 s on the other two, with 857 s for the strength fold of the inference-rule table. The primary evaluation as a whole took about 216 s on four processes. The synthetic study, the leaderboard runs and the sensitivity runs took longer (the synthetic study about 25 minutes). Pilot runs and the analyses added after the first results (listed in the pre-registration record released with the code) used further compute that these figures do not include. Fitting the factor model once per training set and one greedy pass per new model are small next to running a single benchmark.

The raw MTEB scores are the public MTEB results, released under CC0-1.0. The Open LLM Leaderboard per-subtask results are the supplied task data; they declare no licence that we know of. The code uses \texttt{mteb} 2.27.24 (Apache-2.0), NumPy, SciPy, pandas and scikit-learn (BSD-3-Clause) and PyArrow (Apache-2.0); versions are in the lock file released with the code.

The study, including its code, analyses and the text of this paper, was carried out by language-model agents working under a research-group protocol; the method itself uses no language model.
